introduction:
Volumetric occupancy mapping is a core building block of autonomous navigation, and its real-time cost is dominated by the finest resolution at which measurements are integrated and stored [5, 8, 10].
A system that uses centimeter-scale fidelity everywhere also incurs it’s cost everywhere, even though most of a large environment is never on, or even close to, the area the robot will actually navigate on.

This paper turns that observation into a mechanism: allocate resolution by plan-relevance, finely mapping near the plan and coarsely everywhere else, as shown in Fig. 1, according to the diagram presented in Fig. 2.
The natural objection – why not map everything coarse? – is exactly the discriminator our experiments are built around: a uniform coarse map quantizes the terrain the robot must traverse, degrading and over-optimizing the paths the planner reasons about.
Tiering is only worth defending if it avoids that failure while approaching the coarse map’s savings, and on the harder of our two real-world scenarios, that is precisely the trade it achieves.

This paper contributes:

An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

An evaluation methodology isolating resolution allocation as the sole independent variable across six configurations, scored offline under a shared, independent reference map;

A secondary ablation against a plan-free, budget-matched sensor-range baseline.

Section II situates this against prior mapping work; Section III describes the mechanism; Section IV presents the evaluation’s results and discussion, and Section V presents the conclusion and future work.

1. An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

2. An evaluation methodology isolating resolution allocation as the sole independent variable across six configurations, scored offline under a shared, independent reference map;

3. A secondary ablation against a plan-free, budget-matched sensor-range baseline.

Going back to work after having recovered from an upper-limb injury is a delicate moment for workers, because they might have not completely retrieved their full mobility prior to the accident. Collaborative robots and humanoids are progressively appearing in industrial environments and could facilitate this process by adapting their behaviour to the physical capabilities of individual workers [1, 2]. Such adaptations require robots to estimate the user’s residual functional mobility.
With such knowledge, robots can further tune the physical interaction with the user to make their reintegration in the workplace more manageable and personalised.

Additionally, robots have the potential to plan their own motions and collaborate with workers to avoid compensatory movements and take over the workers’ more proper motions related to work activity, because their functional mobility cannot yet be fully reengaged [3, 4]. Although robotic solutions that can account for compensatory movements have been developed in rehabilitation settings [5], a system capable of achieving the same purpose in industrial settings has yet to be designed. Indeed, the few existing works addressing upper-limb impairments in human-robot collaboration primarily substitute the user’s movements with robotic assistance or minimise the need for arm motion altogether [6, 7, 8], rather than explicitly modelling and adapting to the user-specific joint level limitations.

In order to adapt an interaction to a worker’s physical capabilities, the robot requires a quantitative representation of the user’s mobility at the joint level. A joint’s range of motion (ROM) describes its overall admissible angular interval. However, the
portion of that interval available or expressed during a goal-directed activity may be smaller. We refer to this task-available interval as the functional range of motion (fROM). In clinical settings, scores like the Fugl-Meyer’s [9] are used to give a qualitative evaluation of the user’s ROMs. However, when it comes to human-aware motion planning, clinical scores lack in providing quantitative linking to the mobility at the joint level [10], which could be used by a robot to properly tune the interaction according to the specific joint movements to elicit [11]. Furthermore, clinical scores are measured while having patients performing unnatural movements, which could alter the functional ROM (fROM) during more usual work activity. In relation to the upper limb, previous attempts of measuring its ROMs have already been performed in literature. Keyvanian et al. [12] explored an unsupervised learning approach to learn ROM boundary functions, by collecting full-range movements of healthy people, exploring one by one all the possible ROMs of the upper arm. They then defined an impairment index, a high-level quantitative metrics of the user’s impairment that takes into account all the ROMs estimated altogether. However, in this approach, they explored free movements of the arm without focusing on goal-oriented and manipulation tasks [13], having participants extensively move each joint at a time to their fullest extent. Considering the upper limb, the engagement of each degree of freedom (DOF) of it changes from using them one at a time and altogether within a goal-oriented action [14]. Therefore, the fROMs of the user acting in a setting may differ. Because of this, Liu et al. [15] proposed a framework that leveraged information from task-based recordings of people with different ROMs. Upon receiving clinical assessment scores of the ROMs related to the recordings [9], they learned a mapping from the clinical scores to the ROMs, so that the robot could learn the fROMs of a new user by using their clinical scores and without motion tracking. Although effective, this methodology relies on having the clinical scores available and updated. While these could be available at the beginning of the reintegration in the workplace, it can be demanding to summon a clinician again in the future to track and monitor the progress of the recovery outside of the clinical settings. A robot should be able to evaluate the ROMs of the user independently, without having to rely on external human inputs for this information.

Therefore, in this work, we developed a pipeline through which a collaborative robot estimates the impairment coefficients associated with the user’s upper-limb fROM through an active perception process (see Fig. 1). In this, the robotic system repetitively hands over an object to the human user. Based on the user’s kinematics at each handover pose, the belief over the impairment coefficients is updated. The resulting coefficients parameterise the modeled fROM bounds of the considered joints. This process is repeated until the system finds an accurate estimate of the fROMs inspected. No previous learning is requested to perform this estimation. We demonstrated the validity of this approach through an experimental validation in which a participant performed the requested handovers while having their upper arm movements artificially hindered. The contributions of this work are threefold:

a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

an online active perception pipeline where the robot initiates handovers and updates a belief over the user’s functional mobility;

validation of the proposed framework through deployment on a real robotic platform interacting with a participant with enacted impairments.

This paper is structured as follows. Upon having introduced our work (see Section I), Section II describes the human modeling adopted for the problem and the active perception pipeline used for the ROM perception problem. Section III and IV report the experiments carried out and the related results, respectively. Finally, Section V and VI reason about the interpretations of the results and summarise the content of the paper.

1. a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

2. an online active perception pipeline where the robot initiates handovers and updates a belief over the user’s functional mobility;

3. validation of the proposed framework through deployment on a real robotic platform interacting with a participant with enacted impairments.

abstract:
Integrating world models into Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) has emerged as a promising direction, enabling policies to anticipate future dynamics beyond reactive observation-to-action mapping.
However, existing approaches vary simultaneously in their world representations, integration paradigms, and policy backbones, making it difficult to determine what truly matters for effective world model integration.
To disentangle these factors, we categorize existing efforts into four representative paradigms, namely Implicit Future Alignment, Explicit Future Query, MoT Future Expert, and External World Predictor.
We instantiate each within a unified framework and systematically evaluate these paradigms with diverse world representations and policy backbones across in-domain, out-of-domain, long-horizon, dynamic, few-shot, and real-world settings.
Our study yields four main findings:
1) world representation effectiveness is paradigm-dependent, whereas 2D semantic representations offer robust, cross-paradigm gains;
2) no single paradigm is universally optimal, yet the decoupled MoT Future Expert offers competitive overall performance, particularly in OOD robustness;
3) world modeling consistently benefits policies built on standard VLMs, while decoupled designs exhibit greater compatibility with pretrained VLAs;
and 4) world modeling improves performance over action-only baselines across multiple settings, with notable gains on long-horizon and dynamic tasks.
Project page and video demonstrations are available at https://dylanmqs.github.io/WM-in-VLAs/.
